 :adr/repos "com-junkawasaki/root"
 :adr/related "adr-2609092400-the-checkout-was-the-stale-plane-and-a-program-outgrows-a-document, adr-2608136800-checkout-staleness, manifest/session-sync.edn"
 :adr/body "Owner instruction, 2026-09-09: make it a rule that west pins and the like are
synced to latest BEFORE a session.

## What went wrong first

The pure S-expression core was refused by the `kotoba` CLI for days with
\"operation has no admitted lowering\". The language had it. The west pin had it.
The SHARED `amu` CHECKOUT was 200 commits behind that pin, and the kotoba-sema
it pinned was 71 commits behind main.

CLAUDE.md already says `checkout / west pin / repo main` are three different
things. The stale one was the FIRST, and nothing measured it:
`session-start-checkout-staleness` compares a checkout with its own
last-fetched default branch, ranked by how many things READ it -- and a
toolchain repo that nobody `:local/root`s does not rank. The compiler everyone
runs was 200 commits stale and no session-start check printed a line about it.

## The rule

Before a session does anything else, the toolchain checkouts are brought to
their west pins.

  roster     manifest/session-sync.edn
  mechanism  .claude/hooks/session-start-toolchain-pin-sync.cljs (SessionStart)
  manual     nbb .claude/hooks/session-start-toolchain-pin-sync.cljs \"$PWD\" [--dry-run]

It SYNCS rather than warns. This workspace has written down twice that reading
a warning and syncing are different actions, and this session was the proof:
the staleness hook ran, printed its line, and the 200-commit gap stayed. So a
clean checkout is moved to its pin silently -- detached, exactly what west
does -- and only three states are left alone, each with a reason printed:

  * tracked changes            -- someone's WIP is not discarded (CLAUDE.md)
  * unpushed commits on a branch -- a bot's work is not moved out from under it
  * the pinned commit is absent and the fetch did not finish in budget

Untracked files do not block a checkout and are ignored; amu carries 588.

The PIN's own freshness is reported, not acted on: if a pin is behind the
last-fetched origin/main the hook prints the count and
`nbb scripts/west-pin-put.cljs <name> HEAD`. Advancing a pin is a write with a
reachability proof behind it and is not something a session-start hook should
do from a shared checkout.

## Measured on the day it landed

First real run, against the live workspace:

  synced   kotoba-sema, kotoba-native      (behind their pins)
  left     amu           tracked changes
  left     kotoba-lang   commits on bot/migrate-ledger-tick68-20260909
  reported kotoba-script pin 1 behind, osaho pin 2 behind

So both directions were observed on the first run: it moved two checkouts that
were wrong, and it refused two that it had no business touching.

The roster is not \"important repos\" -- it is the ones whose code RUNS when any
other repo is compiled (amu, kotoba-sema, kotoba-kir, kotoba-hir,
kotoba-script, kotoba-wasm, kotoba-native, kotoba-object, osaho, text,
kotoba-lang). A session that reads those stale reads everything else through a
compiler it did not choose.

## Two design points that are easy to get wrong

The pins are read from `origin/main`'s west.yml, not the working tree's. A
stale local manifest as the authority would nail down the very drift this
exists to remove; when origin/main is unreadable the hook falls back and SAYS
it fell back.

A missing roster file, an unreadable manifest, or a budget overrun each print a
line. A silent exit would be indistinguishable from a healthy session, which is
the defect this workspace has now counted fifteen times.
"}]
